From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:50:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] signal: do not count alignment faults toward the fault-loop
 breaker

The Mach handler's livelock breaker (ml385, ml876) diverts a thread to
abort_thread once the same pc faults on the same address 50 times in
a row. An alignment fault (EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN) there is a misaligned
atomic, a guest split lock in code FEX translated: it goes on to the
thread's SIGBUS handler, FEX emulates the access and resumes after it
("Handled unaligned atomic: new pc"), so the same pc and address in a
row is a loop making progress, not a livelock.

Jurassic World Evolution's Wwise bank thread runs such a loop on a
stack variable after it reads the sound engine's init bank, about 9.6 s
after the game starts. The breaker ended the thread on every run since
the game got past its anti-tamper, so the sound engine never finished
its start, nothing fed the engine's job loop, and the game's WatchDog
crashed it on purpose at about 47 s. Alignment faults are no longer
counted, and they leave the breaker's state as it was.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-12-jwe-io-completion.md
Offered-upstream: no; the Mach exception handler is the iOS port's
---
 build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c | 16 ++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
index 29e28b2..fdcabe6 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
@@ -4658,8 +4658,16 @@ skip_reclaim_band: ;
                          * (StikDebug's 8-stop kill was the only breaker, and it
                          * had detached). */
                         if (!fpage) fpage = 1;
-
-                        if (fpage == stuck_page && fault_pc_check == stuck_pc &&
+                        /* PLA-238: an alignment fault (EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN) is not
+                         * counted. It is a misaligned atomic, a guest split lock
+                         * in code FEX translated: FEX emulates it and resumes after
+                         * it, so the same pc and address in a row is a loop making
+                         * progress, not a livelock. Jurassic World Evolution's
+                         * Wwise bank thread runs such a loop on a stack variable,
+                         * and this breaker ended the thread 50 faults in. */
+                        int align_fault = fault_kr == EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN;
+
+                        if (!align_fault && fpage == stuck_page && fault_pc_check == stuck_pc &&
                             (uint64_t)fault_addr != stuck_addr)
                         {
                             /* ml876: same page+pc but a NEW address = progress.
@@ -4675,7 +4683,7 @@ skip_reclaim_band: ;
                                     (unsigned long long)fault_addr);
                             if (pn >= 20000) stuck_n = 49;   /* storm ceiling: next fault breaks */
                         }
-                        if (fpage == stuck_page && fault_pc_check == stuck_pc)
+                        if (!align_fault && fpage == stuck_page && fault_pc_check == stuck_pc)
                         {
                             uint32_t n = __sync_add_and_fetch(&stuck_n, 1);
                             /* ml385 LOOP BREAKER: same page AND same pc 50 times
@@ -4745,7 +4753,7 @@ skip_reclaim_band: ;
                                 }
                             }
                         }
-                        else
+                        else if (!align_fault)
                         {
                             stuck_page = fpage;
                             stuck_pc = fault_pc_check;
